    {
      "text": "If a company is valued at $2B, has ~$1B in cash/inventory... And makes $354M FCF a quarter. \n\nThen returns 30-50% of FCF for dividends next year. \n\nI'd argue it's more of a matter of time before shareholder returns/fundamentals, (especially memory) overpower forced selling. \n\nBut stock prices can look really dislocated from actual performance for periods of time, like now. \n\nSame with photonics, but that's more of a 2027-2028 growth type story with CPO + more optical content/xpu.\n\nLaser companies like $LITE, $AAOI, and potentially $SIVE depending on quals, would likely be printing few years af",
